"""layers.1.mlp.down_proj:4179 -- a sparse lexical detector.

What it does: this component writes a large value into the residual stream on a few
specific words, and a smaller value on the English word "any" when it sits in a
negative or question-like clause. Its activation is near zero almost everywhere.

The program predicts, per token:
  * meat words: 肉 (meat) is by far the strongest trigger (about 4.3); 牛肉, 鸡肉, 肌肉 smaller;
  * hand / mobile phone: 手机 about 1.5, 手 about 0.8, 用手 / 对手 about 0.7;
  * computing: " android" about 1.0, 电脑 about 0.55, 软件 about 0.3;
  * "any": 任何 (Chinese "any") about 1.6; English " any" is graded by context:
    after "without" about 2.8, after a negation (not, n't, no, never, nor, none, nothing)
    within the last few tokens about 0.8, after have/has/there/if/is/do/... about 0.5,
    otherwise about 0.06;
  * a "Title:" colon right after a "<|endoftext|>" boundary: about 4.8 (a rare, very
    strong pattern);
  * the first token of each document: a constant -0.16 (the real value depends on the
    token, which no simple concept captures);
  * everything else: 0.

Why: the variance is dominated by these few triggers. Predicting a constant everywhere
explains nothing; the lexicon plus the "any" negation rule captures most of what is
explainable in plain terms. A per-token table would add more on the training rows but
generalises poorly (held-out token-table R^2 about 0.07).
"""

import re

READS = ()

MEAT = {"肉": 4.3, "牛肉": 1.1, "鸡肉": 0.6, "肌肉": 0.8}
HAND_PHONE = {"手": 0.8, "手机": 1.5, "手機": 0.5, "用手": 0.7, "对手": 0.7}
COMPUTING = {" android": 1.0, "电脑": 0.55, "软件": 0.3}
ANY_ZH = {"任何": 1.6, "有任何": 0.7}
NEGATION = re.compile(r"n't\b|\bnot\b|\bno\b|\bnever\b|\bnor\b|'t\b|\bnone\b|\bnothing\b", re.I)
QUESTION_CUES = {"have", "has", "there", "if", "are", "is", "do", "does", "did", "can", "make", "find"}


def step(carry, prefix, upstream):
    text = prefix.token_strings[-1]
    previous = prefix.token_strings[-2] if len(prefix.token_strings) > 1 else ""
    # first token of a document (the packed row restarts attention and positions here)
    if prefix.position == 0 or prefix.document_ids[-1] != prefix.document_ids[-2]:
        return None, -0.16
    # "<|endoftext|>Title:" boundary
    if text == ":" and previous == "Title" and len(prefix.token_strings) > 2 and prefix.token_strings[-3] == "<|endoftext|>":
        return None, 4.8
    for table in (MEAT, HAND_PHONE, COMPUTING, ANY_ZH):
        if text in table:
            return None, table[text]
    if text == " any":
        if previous.strip() == "without":
            return None, 2.8
        if NEGATION.search("".join(prefix.token_strings[-6:-1])):
            return None, 0.8
        if previous.strip() in QUESTION_CUES:
            return None, 0.5
        return None, 0.06
    return None, 0.0

"""layers.1.mlp.down_proj:4180: a sparse component that fires on the word "hill".

It is silent almost everywhere. Its clearest trigger is the word "hill" (as " Hill",
"hill", " Hills", "hills") and a few H-words near it (" Haw", " Hawks"), where it is
strongly positive, about +2. Weaker positive responses go to H-surnames and place
names such as "Harris" and "Hall". Everything else is predicted as 0.

The program is stateless: it reads only the newest token.
"""

READS = ()

HILL_WORDS = {"Hill", "hill", "Hills", "hills", "Haw", "Hawks"}
HILL_VALUE = 2.2
WEAK_WORDS = {"Harris": 0.9, "Hall": 0.5}


def step(carry, prefix, upstream):
    word = prefix.token_strings[-1].strip()
    if word in HILL_WORDS:
        return carry, HILL_VALUE
    return carry, WEAK_WORDS.get(word, 0.0)

